      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nWhen studying numbers in different numeral systems across different continents (Asia, America - *Abya Yala*, and Africa), we observe that humanity created different symbols to represent counting and that these symbols were different from the alphabet we use in our daily lives.\n\nNow, observe the Roman numerals, also known as Latin numerals, adapted from the Etruscan people, in the following table, compared with other decimal systems (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7be50fd2-2ce2-4d2d-b123-22d6bae50e23",
                     "text": "Keyser, P.",
                     "page": "pp. 544",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "28"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c6f861ac-7b52-4dd4-8e64-e5fb5bc87ad5",
                     "text": "Gregersen, E. (2026, July 27)",
                     "page": "Roman numerals",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "28"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen tableTenBgBlue\"><tr><th>Numeral system</th><th  colspan=\"7\">Numbers</th></tr> <tr><td>Roman</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">I</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">V</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">X</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">L</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">C</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">D</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">M</td> </tr>  <tr><td>Indo-Arabic</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">1</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">5</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">10</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">50</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">100</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">500</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">1000</td> </tr> <tr><td rowspan=\"3\">Mesoamerican Vigesimal</td><td  rowspan=\"3\" style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt1\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt1', '0%')\" alt=\"Maya numeral 1\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_1.png\" /></td><td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt5\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt5', '0%')\" alt=\"Maya numeral 5\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_5.png\" /></td><td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt10\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt10', '0%')\" alt=\"Maya numeral 10\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_10.png\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\" rowspan=\"2\"><img id=\"idt250\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt250', '0%')\" alt=\"Maya numeral 2\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_2.png\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\" rowspan=\"2\"><img id=\"idt5100\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt5100', '0%')\" alt=\"Maya numeral 5\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_5.png\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white; border-bottom: none;\"><img id=\"idt1500\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt1500', '0%')\" alt=\"Maya numeral 1\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_1.png\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white; border-bottom: none;\"><img id=\"idt21000\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt21000', '0%')\" alt=\"Maya numeral 2\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_2.png\" /></td></tr>            <tr style=\"background-color: white;\"><td><img id=\"idt1050\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt1050', '0%')\" alt=\"Maya numeral 5\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_5.png\" /> </td><td><img id=\"idt0100\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt0100', '0%')\" alt=\"Maya numeral 10\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_10.png\" /> </td></tr> <tr style=\"background-color: white;\"><td><img id=\"idt1050\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt1050', '0%')\" alt=\"Maya numeral 10\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_10.png\" /></td><td><img id=\"idt01000\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt01000', '0%')\" alt=\"Maya numeral 0\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_0.png\" /></td><td><img id=\"idt0500\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt0500', '0%')\" alt=\"Maya numeral 0\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_0.png\" /></td><td><img id=\"idt01000\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt01000', '0%')\" alt=\"Maya numeral 0\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_0.png\" /></td></tr></table> </p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Comparison of Roman, Indo-Arabic, and Mesoamerican numbers.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: This reminds me of...</b><br /><br />Looking at the numeral systems in <b>Table 1</b>, what do the Roman numbers remind you of?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_history",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The relationship between peoples\n\nBefore we discuss the possible memories that the numeral system brings us, it is necessary to explain why we are treating the numeral system presented on this page as *Etruscan-Roman*.\n\nAs Paul Keyser mentions, the Etruscans dominated Rome during its real period  (<htdate0> - <htdate1>) (<htcite0>). As a consequence of the ancient relationship between peoples, the Latin alphabet would have its origin in the Etruscan alphabet. And, since numbers were possibly contemporary creations to the alphabet, they would also have been a source of knowledge for the Romans (<htcite0>).\n\nThe fact that we have this knowledge today does not mean that we always knew the relationship between these numbers. Given this, several researchers have proposed theories to explain the reason why the Romans would have 7 symbols for their numbers (<htcite1>).\n\nWith the help of Paul Keyser's work, we will learn a little more about this history and see whether these numbers are the same letters of our alphabet.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4ca46079-38b3-49bc-862d-1840854c9fd9",
                     "text": "Salmon, E.T., et al.",
                     "page": "Acient Rome",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "10"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7be50fd2-2ce2-4d2d-b123-22d6bae50e23",
                     "text": "Keyser, P.",
                     "page": "pp. 538, 541",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "28"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7be50fd2-2ce2-4d2d-b123-22d6bae50e23",
                     "text": "Keyser, P.",
                     "page": "pp. 530 - 543",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "28"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "-753",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "-509",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_alphabet",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### It looks like alphabet, therefore...\n\nThe process of memory construction is not as simple as people thought a few centuries ago. Recent research shows us that memory formation involves several areas of our brain and cannot be limited to a simple process such as visualizing an event, reinforcing it, and storing it. We also cannot limit our memories to short-term and long-term memory (<htcite0>). But this is a recent study. In the past, some proposals about how Roman numerals emerged apparently followed these ideas.\n\nIn his work, Paul Keyser mentions different ideas that associated the Latin alphabet and other alphabets with Etruscan-Roman numbers over a long period of time (<htcite1>). But, before we discuss the problems with these proposals, let us reflect a bit more.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "82ee781f-4d3f-4e98-8c05-47b28e3d3137",
                     "text": "Nadel, L., Hupbach, A., Gomez, R., & Newman-Smith, K.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "29"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7be50fd2-2ce2-4d2d-b123-22d6bae50e23",
                     "text": "Keyser, P.",
                     "page": "pp. 536 - 541",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "28"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: It has the letter, so it is the number</b><br /><br />In Latin, we read a thousand as <i>mille</i>. Since the word starts with <b>M</b> and the number 1000 is represented with <b>M</b>;, is this enough to define how numbers were created?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### A deeper analysis\n\nIf the rule of the reflection is true, then it would be necessary for it to be applied to all numbers. But, in Latin, the number five is *quinque*, meaning it does not start with *V* (<htcite0>).\n\nIf we rule out the letters of the alphabet as the origin of numbers, we need to study other possibilities, which must be proven with texts made by Etruscans and Romans.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7be50fd2-2ce2-4d2d-b123-22d6bae50e23",
                     "text": "Keyser, P.",
                     "page": "pp. 541",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_wide",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### A broader scenario\n\nLet us begin by observing the Etruscan numbers that possibly served as inspiration for Roman numerals (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7be50fd2-2ce2-4d2d-b123-22d6bae50e23",
                     "text": "Keyser, P.",
                     "page": "pp. 544",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen tableTenBgBlue\"><tr><th>Numeral system</th><th  colspan=\"7\">Numbers</th></tr> <tr><td>Roman</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">I</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">V</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">X</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">L</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">C</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">D</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">M</td> </tr>  <tr><td>Indo-Arabic</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">1</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">5</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">10</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">50</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">100</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">500</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">1000</td> </tr>        <tr><td>Etruscan</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">I</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">𐌡</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">𐌢</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">𐌣</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">𐌟</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">&nbsp;</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">⊕</td> </tr> </table> </p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 2</b>: Comparison of Roman, Indo-Arabic, and Etruscan numbers.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Studying the numbers\n\nWe begin by noting that we do not write the Etruscan number 500, since we do not have it in the text format we are using. But it does exist and is represented by the lower half of the Etruscan number 1000.\n\nAccording to Paul Keyser's understanding, Roman numerals would have the following origin (<htcite0>):\n- The origin of Roman numerals are modifications of the original Etruscan numbers;\n- The number 1 (I) represents a unitary mark;\n- The number 10 (𐌢) is the same number used by the Etruscans.\n- The numbers for 5 (𐌡) and 50 (𐌣) are the lower part of the numbers 10 and 100, respectively. When the Romans began to use them, they would have inverted both symbols, and 50 would have undergone an additional transformation, just like 100.\n- For the number 1000, Paul argues that the transformation would also be possible, but a more in-depth study of Latin is needed to understand the stages of transformation.\n- As for the number 500, it would be equivalent to the Etruscan one, but instead of using the lower part of the number as the Etruscans did, we would use the right side of the number 1000.\n\nThis proposal by Paul Keyser was presented after discussing different models proposed to try to explain the origin of Roman numerals, bringing as one of its advantages the connection with comparisons of alphabet signs with proven origins.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7be50fd2-2ce2-4d2d-b123-22d6bae50e23",
                     "text": "Keyser, P.",
                     "page": "pp. 541 - 543",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text, we learned that Latin numbers possibly derive from Etruscan numbers and that an association with the alphabet is a simplification that makes sense in a simple comparison, but with a more in-depth study we can observe problems with the proposal.\n\nFurthermore, we learned that the model of memory association has also undergone transformation over time, moving from a model in which there would be constant reinforcement and memories would be stored, to a more complex model involving different areas of the brain that help construct our memories.\n\nGiven all this information, we close with an inner reflection for the readers.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: I limit myself because I do not understand</b><br /><br />Is it possible that you have clung to a simple answer because it seemed to make sense, but you have not studied it more deeply?<br /><br />Might people accept easy answers based on what knowledge they have because they do not have time or do not know how to seek more knowledge?<br /><br />In this text, we learned that researchers have shown that short-term and long-term memory models, as well as the origin of Roman numerals from the alphabet, cannot be considered correct due to the limitations they have. Can we say that people who only receive limited models, or no model at all, during their lives regarding this and other questions are restricted in their knowledge?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
   "exercise_v2": [
      {
         "question": "Is it possible that a people acquires knowledge from another people and adapts it according to their local needs?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "This occurs all over the planet. As contact between cultures occurred, peoples exchanged mathematical knowledge, agricultural knowledge, and even beliefs, whether in a friendly manner or imposed on the other."
      },
      {
         "question": "Can we firmly state that Roman numerals originate from the letters that name them and therefore originated from the alphabet?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "No",
         "additionalInfo": "If such information were true, all seven digits would start with the letter that names each number, something that definitely never happened. Given this scenario, Paul Keyser's proposal that we studied, of origin from Etruscan numbers, is more likely."
      }
   ],
